# Enter the Universal Library

Every point in this experience begins with one rule: a volume has $n$ positions, and each position takes one of $q$ symbols. Start by changing those two numbers and watching a finite universe appear.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The growth is exponential, but its information content is linear in $n\log_2 q$. The first visualization makes both perspectives comparable.

{{visualization:0}}

## Give Every Volume an Address

A book is also a base-$q$ numeral. Use the workbench to rank any valid word, then inspect the precise forward-and-inverse algorithm.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why ranking is bijective</summary>
Every integer from $0$ through $q^n-1$ has one fixed-length base-$q$ expansion after leading zeros are allowed. Positional evaluation and repeated division by $q$ are inverse operations, so the correspondence is one-to-one and onto.
</details>

## Probability Requires an Acceptance Rule

A fixed target has probability $1/q^n$, while any explicit checker has probability $|A|/q^n$. Explore an exact finite example and compare the residue classes.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the modeling principle</summary>
“Meaningful” is not a counting predicate until an encoding and deterministic acceptance rule are supplied. Complexity of one proof does not determine how many equivalent texts the checker accepts.
</details>

## Overlap Is Not a Random-Access Table

A [de Bruijn sequence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Bruijn_sequence) displays every length-$n$ word once as a cyclic window. Generate one below. Its overlap is optimal for sequential enumeration, but locating an input word is a separate inverse-index problem.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## The Catalog Capacity Wall

A library with $M=q^n$ books has $M^M$ possible complete address tables. Since $N$ storage books have only $M^N$ states, all tables fit only when $N\ge M$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the complete counting proof</summary>
An injective storage format must have at least as many states as the objects stored. Thus $M^M\le M^N$. For $M\ge2$, exponentiation is strictly increasing, so $M\le N$. Directly storing one address per book attains equality.
</details>

## Where to Go Next

The sharp worst-case bound leaves room for structure. Semantic subsets may compress; cyclic enumerations may support better inverse lookup; and error correction changes the threshold. For background, see resources on [information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_theory), [the pigeonhole principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeonhole_principle), and [Kolmogorov complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov_complexity).
